Robin Hanson wrote 8/11/94: <It's been ten years since I really studied
philosophy of science (M.A. U.Chicago '84), but I did learn a few things.
Amateurs who want to discuss a philosophical question, especially on the net
where folks are tempted to post as fast as they can type, are well advised to
try to emulate the cognitive style of professional philosophers.  Please, think
a lot, type a little, and try to be as precise as possible when you make claims.
Explain what you mean by common but contentious terms like "rational" or
"moral", rather than assuming we know what you mean.  There are many great
philosophical questions worthy of discussion, but unfortunately most net
discussions of them are not worth the bother.>

I wish to thank Robin for posting such a fine example of the debilitating kind
of credentialism that closes off potential sources of truth.  Predictably,
someone responded with credentials in response instead of plain speech that
illuminates:
Ed Regis wrote 8/12/94: <For the record I have a BA, MA, and PhD in philosophy,
was a tenured professor of philosophy for 15+ years before resigning to become a
full-time science writer, and have published a fair amount within the discipline
of philosophy.>

I salute "professional" philosophers who regain their "amateur" status in flight
from the PC-ridden universities.  They'll find more truth, do more good out of
the leftist cloisters than in them.  Discussing how many angels can dance on the
head of a deconstructed pin seems a waste of time for grown-ups.

I also agree with Ed's comment "As for defining common terms like "rational" and
"moral," I suppose I would be happy to accept any ordinary dictionary
definition...  Usually, and despite appearances, nothing special hinges on such
terms, and people pretty much know what they mean."  I also agree with Max's
comment "Even if he wasn't formerly a professor of philosophy (and so just maybe
has an idea what he's saying)..."  I'm just in an agreeable mood today.

I agree with Nietzsche about the role of a philosopher (I don't know if he was
amateur or professional):  "To correct what is wrong, and strengthen the right,
and raise what is holy."  I also agree with Wittgenstein about exercising care
with language (I don't know if he was amateur or professional): "Philosophy must
not in any way, however slight, interfere with the ordinary use of language; in
the end, philosophy can only describe it.  ... It leaves everything as it is."

Redefinitions of meaning, truth, rationality, etc. by "professional"
philosophers frequently are political attempts to secure Gnostic power.  The
logical positivists do this when they hold that "to state what experience would
make a proposition true is to state its meaning," the deconstructionists do this
through destroying others' sense of truth in favor of their viewpoint and
rationalists do this by pronouncing some propositions as rational and some as
irrational.  There is nothing wrong with this, it is simply imposing themselves
on everyone else, I just wish they were more honest about their motivations
instead of hiding behind the cloak of "professionalism."

My experience on the Net with philosophical discussions has been more positive
than Robin's.  While discussions with relativists haven't yielded much
interesting evidence, mostly because their uncertainty goes hand-in-hand with
their lack of conviction, I have run across some very interesting
generalizations from thoughtful people reflecting over their whole life's
experiences.  Very fruitful pieces of evidence to help draw even greater
generalizations about the transhuman condition.

