In <9408121224.tn1008166@aol.com>, EdRegis@aol.com writes:
> grigsby@agames.com (Thu, Aug 11, 1994 1:36 AM EST) writes:
[...]
> >This is the core of
> >my challenge, which is that _you cannot prove the existence >of objective
> >reality through your subjective perception_.
> 
> You can prove that some statements are more than assumptions by looking at
> the objective evidence that exists for those statements, in the form of
> sense-experience, experiment, and other facts and data.  Saying that "you
> cannot prove the existence of objective reality through your subjective
> perception" begs the question: it assumes that the only thing available to us
> is "subjective perception," which is false.  
> 
[...]
Actually, this is the starting point of Greek philosophy, wasn't it?
The most radical skeptics like Epaminides (who would hardly even talk
to anyone), insisted that no one could know anything because your only
inputs are sensory and no one can prove the objective world is not an
illusion. Elaborate and oddly self-consistent as illusions go, but
nevertheless ...

For example:

"Another notable school of thought that appeared in the late 4th and
early 3rd centuries BC is skepticism. Founded by Pyrrho of Elis, it
asserts that humans cannot know anything for certain. No one can ever
be sure that what is perceived by the senses is real or only an
illusion. The skeptical view did not make much headway at the time,
but it endured to reach new heights in the work of David Hume in the
18th century. It is one of the most radical positions taken in
epistemology."

Compton's Encyclopedia, Online Edition; downloaded from America Online
8/12/94

I'm *not* siding with the Sophomores here, just pointing out that
you can't just dismiss radical skepsis, waving it away as false; you
have to explicitly take some kind of step to get beyond it. This has
been done in various ways, including agreeing to an axiom that 
denies it (a la Rand).

Hell, if the radical skepsis is true, then why try to communicate
anything? Radical solipsism follows from it -- I think, therefore you
exist :-)


You guys are making my head hurt.

